How do I center a div in CSS?:
Below is a quick‑reference cheat‑sheet for **centering a `<div>` (or any block‑level element)** using modern CSS.  
Feel free to copy‑paste the snippets, tweak the names, and drop them into your own project.

---

## 1️⃣ Horizontal centering (most common)

| Method | Why it works | CSS |
|--------|--------------|-----|
| `margin: 0 auto;` | The element’s left & right margins are set to the same value; the remaining space is divided equally. | ```css
.center‑h {
  width: 50%;          /* or any fixed width */
  margin: 0 auto;
}
``` |
| `display: flex; justify-content: center;` | Flex container distributes space evenly; the child is centered. | ```css
.parent { display: flex; justify-content: center; }
.child   { /* no special width needed */ }
``` |
| `text-align: center` on the parent | Works for inline‑block or inline elements. | ```css
.parent { text-align: center; }
.child  { display: inline-block; }
``` |
| `display: grid; place-items: center;` | Grid centers in both directions; easy one‑liner. | ```css
.parent { display: grid; place-items: center; }
``` |

> **Tip:** If you only need horizontal centering and the element has a fixed width, `margin: 0 auto` is the most lightweight approach.

---

## 2️⃣ Vertical centering

| Method | Why it works | CSS |
|--------|--------------|-----|
| Flexbox (`align-items: center`) | Aligns flex items along the cross‑axis. | ```css
.parent { display: flex; align-items: center; height: 200px; }
``` |
| Grid (`place-items: center`) | Same as above but with grid. | ```css
.parent { display: grid; place-items: center; height: 200px; }
``` |
| Absolute positioning + transform | Centers relative to its positioned ancestor. | ```css
.parent { position: relative; height: 200px; }
.child  { position: absolute; top: 50%; left: 50%; transform: translate(-50%, -50%); }
``` |
| Table / Table‑cell | Legacy but still works in older browsers. | ```css
.parent { display: table; width: 100%; height: 200px; }
.child  { display: table-cell; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; }
``` |

---

## 3️⃣ Full‑screen (both horizontal & vertical)

```html
<div class="fullscreen-center">
  <div class="content">Centered content</div>
</div>
```

```css
.fullscreen-center {
  /* 1️⃣ Flexbox solution (recommended) */
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center;  /* horizontal */
  align-items: center;      /* vertical */
  height: 100vh;            /* full viewport height */
  /* optional: background, padding, etc. */
}

.fullscreen-center .content {
  /* style the inner box as you wish */
}
```

> **Why Flexbox?**  
> - Works in all modern browsers.  
> - No need for extra wrappers or special widths.  
> - Handles dynamic content and resizing gracefully.

---

## 4️⃣ Common pitfalls & gotchas

| Issue | Fix |
|-------|-----|
| `margin: 0 auto;` not centering | Ensure the element has a **fixed width** or a width less than the parent. |
| `display: flex` on parent but child still off‑center | Check for `margin` or `padding` on the child that might push it. |
| Vertical centering with `line-height` | `line-height` only works for single‑line text. Use flex/grid for block content. |
| Older browsers (IE11) | Flexbox works in IE11, but `place-items` (grid) does not. Use flex or absolute + transform for IE11 support. |
| `position: absolute` needs positioned ancestor | Set `position: relative` (or absolute/fixed) on the parent, otherwise it positions relative to the page. |

